WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
- [Host] So up next we have my colleague

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.260
and friend Dhanya Sridhar.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.690
She's an assistant professor
at the University of Montreal

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:10.170
CIFAR AI chair, faculty at Mila.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.613
Her research focus is on explaining why.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.070
She's focused on causality
and machine learning

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.260
and working on AI systems,

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.520
and she's here to talk about
causality and deep learning.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.930
Take it away, Dhanya.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.040
- [Dhanya] Okay, awesome.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.210
Can everybody hear me.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.650
Okay, fantastic.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.790
Okay, so indeed, today's
talk will be about exploring

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.430
the intersection of
causality and deep learning.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.920
So let me just kind of tell
you about the objectives

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:41.910
that I have for us today.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.030
So if there are three
things that we achieve

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.500
during the course of this talk,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.650
I would hope that they
are that we all gain

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.600
a little bit of familiarity
with the language of causality,

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.080
that we get a sense of
where deep learning offers

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.390
opportunities to improve
the field of causality,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.430
and then vice versa, as well,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.573
and then finally,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.320
I would also like us to
understand what are kind of unique

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.120
challenges that we face,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.980
technical challenges in particular,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.220
when we try to combine deep
learning and causality, okay?

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.650
So this is what we're going to try to do.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:17.940
And to start,

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.210
I want to begin with a motivating example

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.390
that I hope will illustrate
that we all probably already

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.810
have somewhat strong
intuitions about causality.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.960
So to begin with that,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.980
let's pretend like we're
all decision making people

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.980
and we want to know how to write
customer service complaints

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.470
in order to get quick responses, okay?

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.730
So what do we do?

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.050
We want to understand how,

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:49.440
we want to build a policy
for how to write complaints

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.400
that get fast replies,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.670
so we gather a data set of
customer service complaints

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:57.000
and their labeled response times, okay?

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.680
So such a data set exists.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.770
In the US, there's the Consumer
Finance Protection Bureau,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.530
where people can lodge complaints

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.460
about a variety of consumer
and financial products,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:12.480
and they also record how timely
this complaint was resolved.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.650
So yes, this angry complaint
is a real complaint,

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.450
so you can have a look at it.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.840
And let's say what we do is
we fit a classifier, right?

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.330
So we fit a classifier that takes text,

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:25.950
these complaints as input

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:30.950
and predicts which features
are useful for fast replies.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.730
And let's say what we did
after our analysis is learned

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.250
that angry complaints
turned out to be predictive

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.120
of fast replies.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.050
So let me ask all of you a
question with a show of hands,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.230
how many of you feel convinced
that you would now just adopt

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.240
this policy of writing angry complaints

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.440
and emails all the time
to get fast replies?

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.173
All right.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:57.270
Okay, maybe some people just
need to vent their frustration.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:58.770
That's fine.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:03.420
But a lot of us had an intuition
perhaps that this analysis

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.030
did not answer the question
that we wanted to answer,

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:10.230
which was how should I change my behavior

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.990
and expect to see this change in the world

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.190
where I get faster replies?

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.130
That's a question about
interventions and their effects.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.010
And predictions don't distinguish

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.430
these interventional queries, right?

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.700
What predictions do
instead is they capture

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.400
all possible associations in a system

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.930
and they don't distinguish between

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.060
the causal impact of a variable

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.340
and just associations between variables.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.990
So in this case, for example,

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.510
maybe this is the true underlying model,

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.540
that there's something
like topics that affect

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.260
both whether or not you
write an angry complaint

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.470
and how quickly those
complaints are resolved.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.310
So, for example,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.340
we actually found in this
dataset that when the complaints

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.280
are about financial products like banks,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.720
the complaints are both angry

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.280
and they're actually resolved

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.490
at a higher rate of timeliness.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.940
So these kinds of spurious
associations exist

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.270
and predictions are not immune
to capturing them, okay?

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:12.610
So we need a language that
allows us to talk about

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.530
the effects of making
manipulations to the world.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.530
And that is what causality is all about.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:23.970
And let me tell you a little
bit about when we see causality

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.620
in the real world when we need it,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.190
just to kind of motivate
that this is a topic that is

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.970
foundational to science,
to decision-making,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:37.823
to understanding bias and many more ideas.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.360
So in keeping with the
theme of science here,

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.340
so, for example,

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.980
when we want to measure the
effectiveness of therapeutics,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.600
when we want to understand, for example,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.040
whether a vaccine

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.193
or a new drug actually has
a causal effect on recovery,

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.660
this is a causal question
that we need to answer.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.090
In order to gain better understanding

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:01.800
of biological mechanisms,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.640
we need to understand
how molecular variables

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.510
have a causal impact on
other downstream pathways

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:12.003
and other macromolecules.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.470
Now, when we come to
decision making, for example,

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.960
causality has already been useful as a way

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.300
of actually finding evidence of biases.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.950
So, for example,

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.740
there was a really interesting
study that looked at

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.070
daylight savings time that introduces

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:33.450
a kind of random variation
in when it gets dark

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:35.580
to show that in fact it seems like

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.703
there is a racial bias with police stops.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:44.070
Moreover, it is a useful
language, as well,

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.100
to think about mitigating
harm from non-causal

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.190
spurious correlations that our large

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:53.190
machine learning systems are
very prone to memorizing.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:54.420
So, for example,

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.970
this is a real prompt that was given to,

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.470
I believe, stable diffusion,

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:01.650
and you ask it to produce a
nurse sitting in a white room

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.740
and you see that it really
amplifies maybe biases

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.690
in our real world, right?

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:10.440
So now you just have a bunch
of female presenting nurses

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.470
in this set of images.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:17.580
And the language that
we need to understand

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:21.630
what differentiates these
kinds of spurious associations

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.500
from associations that are
potentially more stable,

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.500
causality can provide a very useful one.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.190
In that same vein,

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.970
this idea of what remains
stable versus what doesn't

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:40.970
is also a powerful tool
that will be useful

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.250
when we think about generalizing
to out of distribution,

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.580
so to new distributions that
are not our training data

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.470
when we train our deep learning models,

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:53.310
and perhaps these new
distributions are consequence of

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.710
unseen interventions
that the world is taking.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:58.320
So understanding how we can be robust

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.630
to those kinds of unseen interventions,

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.060
causality once again will
provide a useful framework,

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.030
and we'll see this
specifically in this talk.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:09.090
So now that I've kind of motivated

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.850
when causality is applicable,

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:13.650
we've kind of seen that
we already all have

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:14.970
a bit of an intuition,

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:17.790
let's kind of get into the
nuts and bolts a little bit.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:19.860
So to ground this,

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.710
I'm going to present a
causal graphical model here.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:23.543
And the example,

00:07:23.543 --> 00:07:26.280
the running example will
be this clinical setting

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.670
where there's a treatment of interest

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.100
whose causal effect we're
interested in analyzing.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.963
That will be the receipt of a drug.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:39.390
We have some variables that
affect the treatment of interest

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:40.950
as well as our outcome of interest,

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.330
which will be recovery,

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:45.000
and these variables that
affect both the treatment

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.033
and outcome will be the
patient's health status, okay?

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:53.430
So causal models like standard
directed graphical models

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:56.610
tell us how to factorize the
observed data distribution.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:00.210
They tell us what kinds of
independencies are encoded

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.940
in the distribution of
these three variables.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:06.243
And their sort of superpower, though,

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:12.590
is that they also, using a
shared set of parameters,

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.810
they entail what are called
interventional distributions.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.280
So, for example,

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.760
this particular interventional
distribution tells us that

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.140
what we do is we give every single person

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.580
the particular drug, okay?

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:31.170
So we set the random
variable T to value little t,

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:34.770
and therefore there is
no longer a distribution

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.780
over this treatment random variable.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:38.043
It's just a delta spike.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.810
So you can imagine this as
we've decided that no matter

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.220
who comes into the doctor's office,

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.450
we are going to give
them this therapy, okay?

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:52.560
But notice crucially that

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.870
these two different
families of distributions,

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.270
they share all other factors, okay?

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.610
Everything else remains invariant.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.710
So that is to say, for example,

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:05.940
the biological mechanisms
that drive recovery don't vary

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.730
regardless of whether
we give people the drug

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:10.410
or they choose to take the drug, right?

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:13.140
So no matter how the value of t gets set,

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:17.370
this causal mechanism stays
exactly the same, okay?

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:18.810
And to kind of keep going with this,

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.360
to illustrate one more
interventional distribution,

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:25.050
we can think of an
interventional distribution

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:28.920
in which we change the causal
mechanism of recovery itself.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.140
So we do some intervention.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:32.580
Let's say we have a mouse model.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.550
We maybe give the mouse antibiotics

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:37.290
and seed it with different bacteria

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.303
just changing its system fundamentally.

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:43.830
Notice that what an
intervention means graphically

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:48.830
is that we sever that
variable's links to its parents,

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:50.070
and that kind of makes sense, right?

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.490
We no longer allow that
variable to take on a value

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:55.260
by the natural mechanisms.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:59.160
We go in and we intervene and
we set it to a constant, okay?

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:00.660
So this is just the,

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.540
this is basically in
some sense in a nutshell

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.510
all you really need to start
thinking about causality.

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:10.023
So we have this idea of the do operator.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.680
Mathematically what it means
is that we're going to modify

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.610
the graph in this particular
way by removing incoming edges.

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:20.220
And then we have this invariance property

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.890
where every other mechanism
stays exactly the same

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.590
across the family of distributions

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:27.630
that a causal model entails.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:28.890
That's it.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:32.550
So now what kinds of questions
can we ask now that we have

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:33.753
this language of do?

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:39.180
So going to this clinical
setting once again,

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:44.180
a very common causal effect
that we often analyze from data

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.770
is the average treatment effect.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:48.990
And what the average
treatment effect is asking us

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:53.070
is the difference in average outcome

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.140
between two interventional distributions,

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.350
one where you go in and
you give everybody the drug

00:10:58.350 --> 00:10:59.880
and one where you go in

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:03.690
and you prevent everybody
from taking the drug, okay?

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:07.200
So, but mathematically
it's not that special.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.220
It's just looking at the
contrast between the means

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.253
of two univariate distributions, okay?

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.580
But what's special is that these are

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:20.160
interventional distributions
that are defined

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.160
after you modify your graph in
the way that I talked about.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.180
Now, we can be a bit fancier.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:30.600
We can ask about conditional
average treatment effects.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.340
What this is a function.

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:37.340
It takes as well a
variable Z to condition on.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:39.840
And for every value of that variable,

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:43.020
you can understand the causal effect,

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:45.600
the ATE for that variable setting.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.490
So, for example,

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:52.490
for every single value of
little z, you can have an ATE.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.150
So you get a function

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:55.350
and this function is called

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:59.370
the conditional average
treatment effect or CATE, okay?

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.290
So for the rest of this talk,

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:02.550
I'm going to stick with the ATE

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:03.810
just because it's a bit simpler,

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:06.663
but it's conceptually
the same with the CATE.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.440
We want to ask, okay, well,

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:12.783
how do we even draw causal inferences?

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:17.010
Sometimes you can sample from
interventional distributions,

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:20.430
maybe if you can do
experiments like in biology

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.290
where you can literally
manipulate the system,

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:24.810
you can sample from these
interventional distributions.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.540
But what if we cannot?

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:28.980
And moreover, in any case,

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:33.000
you can't both simultaneously
give somebody the drug

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.740
and not give somebody the drug, right?

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:38.070
So we need a way of doing
inference when we can't actually

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.193
sample these interventional
distributions directly.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:44.010
So our kind of instinct would be, well,

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:47.193
what can we do with the observed
data distribution, right?

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:51.120
Unfortunately, we can't just
look at the correlations

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:54.210
between T, our treatment
of interest, and Y,

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:55.590
because we can see here, right?

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:59.100
There's two pathways
that carry associations

00:12:59.100 --> 00:12:59.970
between T and Y.

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.340
One is the actual causal effect,

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.590
and then the other is this pathway

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:08.430
via the confounding variable Z, okay?

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:13.430
And we say that these Z variables

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:15.243
confound the causal effect.

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:20.310
And so, okay, so what we know
now is observed correlations

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:23.490
are not going to be our answer
to identify the causal effect,

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.520
but let's kind of just
take one of the terms

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:27.660
in the average treatment effect.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:30.540
Let's look at the left term

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.210
and let's just break it
down a little bit, okay?

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:36.090
So the first thing we're
going to do is apply

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:38.850
the law of iterated expectation.

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:41.490
The next thing I'm going
to do is just expand out

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:45.840
the outer expectation over
the treatment variable.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.060
And when I do this, I
notice something peculiar,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.030
which is that in the do distribution,

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:52.830
because there's no more randomness

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:54.210
in this random variable T,

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:57.723
because whenever I'm doing T equals one,

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:01.053
T cannot possibly be equal to zero,

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:04.233
the second term just goes away.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:06.390
And then finally,

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:09.330
because I said that every other mechanism

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:11.460
between the do distribution

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:13.740
and the observed
distribution is conserved,

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.420
there's this invariance,

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:18.300
we end up with this
expression at the very end

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:20.403
that's only in terms of the observed data.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:23.250
So there's a little bit of math here,

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:24.930
but let me just tell you
what the takeaway is,

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:27.810
which is that we can do
the same for the other term

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.280
in the ATE,

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:30.510
move things around,

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:33.420
and what we'll get is this
really interesting result

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:37.080
that says that in this
graph when Z is observed,

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.000
when the confounders are observed,

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.570
the observed data
distribution actually gives us

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:43.403
the causal effect.

00:14:43.403 --> 00:14:44.430
There's a way to calculate it

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:46.860
without any interventional samples,

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:49.260
but via this formula
that I just showed you,

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:52.530
which is called causal
adjustment or G formula

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:54.030
or adjustment formula.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:55.593
It goes by many names.

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:59.790
So this is our first contact

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:02.220
with this idea of identifiability

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.650
from non-interventional sources of data.

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:07.110
So there's a way to
calculate causal effects

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:09.660
even if you cannot do those
interventions directly.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:16.230
But alas, what if Z
was not fully observed?

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.090
Then do we still get to apply the formula

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:21.750
and get a unique answer
back for the causal effect?

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:23.760
Unfortunately, no,

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:27.120
because now I'm going to again just write

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:28.470
the adjustment formula,

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:29.580
but expand it a little bit,

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:32.340
and we'll see that this effect is

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:34.440
what we call non-identifiable.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:37.500
What that just simply
means is that there are

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:42.500
infinitely many choices
that we can pick for P of Z

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:44.670
and also the causal mechanism

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:48.540
that don't change the
distribution of the data at all.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:51.480
They don't change P of T and Y at all.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:56.130
That will yield completely
different causal effects, okay?

00:15:56.130 --> 00:16:00.510
So unfortunately, when Z is not observed,

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:04.410
we cannot use this
adjustment formula anymore.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:07.060
But happily, there's
other things we can do

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:10.110
if we can get other kinds of data.

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:14.670
So a common example that
I suspect a lot of you

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:18.150
are familiar with at least
is randomized control trials.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:21.930
So sometimes what we can
do is randomly assign

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:25.770
the treatment variable
with some probability

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:28.770
that does not depend on the confounder Z.

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.350
The most obvious thing to
do is to just flip a coin,

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:32.183
a fair coin,

00:16:32.183 --> 00:16:34.890
and give people the drug or
not give them the drug, right?

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:38.730
And this is one of the
driving forces by which we do

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:41.550
causal inference for clinical settings,

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:46.550
for macroeconomics, policy
assessment and evaluation.

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:48.960
This is kind of the bread and butter.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:53.960
So here, because this randomization mimics

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:57.540
the exact structure of the
interventional distribution,

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:00.420
what we can do is in order to calculate

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.060
the causal effect of doing T,

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:05.010
we just have to condition on T

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:08.910
and look at the average value
of the outcome variable, okay?

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:10.920
So that's one thing we can do.

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:13.050
There is a whole field, as well,

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.960
on quasi-randomization
where we cannot maybe

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:18.210
directly randomize the treatment variable,

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:21.720
but there is some other
what we call instrument

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:26.700
that induces random variation
in the treatment variable

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:28.620
without actually affecting Y at all.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:32.550
So you can think of this as another source

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:35.970
of giving you randomization
without you explicitly

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:37.890
randomizing the assignment
of the treatment.

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:42.450
And with IV, instrumental
variables, sorry,

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.550
things get a bit more complicated

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:46.140
in how you estimate the causal effect.

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:48.030
So I'm not going to kind of go into

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:50.130
the technical details of that,

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:52.413
but this is a field
that you can read about.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:56.523
Yes, is there a question?

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:03.413
Yes.

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:16.110
Okay, there's a question
about if these are DAGs

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:17.850
and if we're doing Bayesian inference.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:20.640
So there's no Bayesian inference
happening here just yet.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:25.170
So what we're doing here is we have

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:27.150
an underlying graphical model, agreed,

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:27.983
and it's a DAG.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:31.860
When we write down a causal
quantity that we care about

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.360
like the ATE,

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:36.990
we can express those distributions

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:38.940
that we want to sample from, for example,

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.130
E of Y do t equals one,

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:44.430
by removing some edges from
the DAG that we began with.

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:47.310
That becomes our new DAG
that we wish to estimate

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:48.870
this quantity in.

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:52.500
And you can't do it literally
by looking at the conditional

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:54.720
distribution in the observed data,

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:57.810
but you can do it with
this adjustment formula.

00:18:57.810 --> 00:18:59.850
Okay, good.

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:04.850
So this was when Z was
just not observed fully

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:11.160
and we had to rely on other kinds of data.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:12.810
But now let me ask a question.

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:16.110
What if the confounders
aren't directly observed,

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:18.360
but we have some other measurements

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:20.010
or some other data that gives us a clue

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:21.840
as to what they are, okay?

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:24.360
So let me motivate that
with a concrete example.

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:28.320
Suppose that in the case of now text,

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:32.130
let's say I want to understand
what is the causal effect

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:33.570
on getting your paper accepted

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.270
if you have a theorem in your paper, okay?

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:40.050
So here the treatment of
interest is including a theorem

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:41.670
in your paper or not.

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:46.350
The outcome of interest is
acceptance at a conference.

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.660
And here the confounders may be

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:51.300
subject matter of the paper, right?

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:54.180
So what you write about
would obviously affect

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:56.970
both whether or not you
put a theorem in your paper

00:19:56.970 --> 00:20:00.660
and also it likely affects
the rates of acceptance

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.033
because we know that
differs across fields.

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:07.500
We don't literally get
to observe the subject

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.480
of every single paper in our dataset.

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:11.883
But let's say we also
get a paper abstract.

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:17.130
So this text information
clearly carries information

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:19.740
about what the subject is, okay?

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:21.840
So that's a setting that we're in.

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:24.240
And notice there's a key assumption here,

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:29.190
which is that the text we're
going to assume does contain

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:31.233
all of the confounding information.

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:37.413
We can, I'm saying this kind
of in a more informal way.

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:41.040
The paper here that I'm presenting

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:42.990
makes it more mathematically precise.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:46.380
But suppose that another
way to think about this

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.690
is that we could have used the full text

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:50.223
to do that causal adjustment,

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:53.913
but why would we maybe
not want to do that?

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:57.930
It's because the text is
high dimensional, for one,

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:01.650
and it contains a lot
of nuisance information

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:04.440
that is not pertinent to
the causal inference problem

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:07.890
that if we adjust for
our effect estimation

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:10.230
becomes very high variance.

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:13.050
It becomes statistically
inefficient, right?

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:17.460
So what we would like is a
representation of the text.

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:19.800
We would like to use ideally
deep learning methods

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:23.700
to recover some numerical
representation of the text

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:27.030
that contains all of the
confounding information,

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:28.950
but nothing else, right?

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:33.950
So we want to kind of have
minimal confounding representation

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:36.930
that we extract from the text, okay?

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.930
So let me kind of build up how we do this

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:42.810
and some intuitions behind the
desiderata that we lay out.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:47.340
So let's just kind of be
more straightforward and say,

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:49.800
well, what if I just apply
my favorite representation

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:51.630
learning method to the text

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:54.903
and I learn some embeddings
that capture the text well?

00:21:56.820 --> 00:21:58.110
Couple things can go wrong, right?

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:00.900
So one thing that can
go wrong is depending on

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:02.583
which objective you use,

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:07.200
this representation that you
learn may fail to recover

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:08.850
all of the confounding information.

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:11.280
Maybe it, whatever objective you set up,

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:14.520
maybe it's a contrastive
objective that prefers to learn

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:16.080
the noisy parts of the text

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:17.780
rather than the confounding parts.

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:22.710
The other thing that can go
wrong is, again, naively,

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:25.830
the representation that
you learn has no incentive

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:29.160
to throw away the irrelevant
information, right?

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:31.320
It's going to learn both the confounding

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:32.640
as well as all the noise.

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:35.043
There's no reason for
it to discard the noise.

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:40.143
So here's the desiderata that we lay out.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:44.610
First we want to learn a mapping from text

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:48.990
to an embedding that knows how
to reconstruct X well, okay?

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:51.570
So that is going to
encourage it to capture

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.673
as much information as possible.

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:57.240
Then we're going to use the
graph structure to regularize.

00:22:57.240 --> 00:22:58.380
So what do we do?

00:22:58.380 --> 00:23:03.380
We train the representation
to predict both T and Y,

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:08.190
because the confounding
information is uniquely the thing

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:10.020
in this model that it can predict

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:11.640
both the treatment and outcome.

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:13.800
The noise is just not going
to have any information

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:15.400
about the treatment and outcome.

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:21.860
And so what we do is build the system

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:25.470
that has some reconstruction objective

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:27.330
and then has these two prediction terms

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:30.660
to encourage capturing just
the confounding information.

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:31.980
So let me give you,

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:33.673
this is a general purpose framework.

00:23:33.673 --> 00:23:38.010
You can slot in your favorite
text representation method,

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:41.760
but we'll give an example here using BERT.

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:46.140
So what we did was we trained BERT to,

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:47.820
we fine tuned BERT, rather,

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:52.050
to predict the treatment label
for every single document

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:53.220
in our corpus

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:56.160
and also the expected outcome,

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:58.530
and we did this for two groups,

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:01.620
the group of documents
that have the treatment,

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:03.120
that have a theorem in them,

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:04.620
and separately the group of documents

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:06.570
that don't have a theorem in them.

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:08.700
So we have three prediction heads

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:12.300
and jointly we continue to fine tune

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:17.100
using the masked token loss,

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:19.260
which in BERT is just a proxy

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:21.063
for reconstructing the text well.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:25.290
So this way we do this
trade off between learning

00:24:25.290 --> 00:24:26.910
as much information as we can

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:29.610
so that we make sure we capture Z

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:32.013
and then throw away
everything that's not Z.

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:33.953
Yeah.

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:51.120
Yeah, the question is about
what if X contains noise?

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:55.230
Doesn't the objective keep the noise?

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:56.460
Well, I mean, of course, indeed,

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:59.940
that's the first desiderata
is indeed to train

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:01.470
to keep as much information

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:03.570
that's predictive of the text as possible.

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:06.240
But the second desiderata

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:09.480
is a constraint that regularizes, right?

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:12.630
That discourages learning anything

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:15.810
that is not predictive of
the treatment and outcome.

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:17.610
So you end up with this
trade-off, of course,

00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:20.550
that you have to deal with,

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:22.290
like we often do when we have

00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:24.360
a lot of constrained optimization,

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:28.353
but that is a separate problem.

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:33.750
Okay, so unfortunately,

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:36.600
evaluating these kinds of methods is hard

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:39.480
because any causal inference
suffers from the problem

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:42.660
that we typically don't
have for some dataset

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:45.030
the average treatment
effect of the treatment

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:47.460
of interest lying around,

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:52.170
so what we do in this case is
a semisynthetic evaluation.

00:25:52.170 --> 00:25:54.540
So we took the period corpus,

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:58.710
which has known paper abstracts.

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:00.660
There's a label for
whether there's a theorem

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:02.070
in the paper or not,

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:04.830
and there's also a label,

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:07.620
there's a covariate for
whether there's a buzzy word

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:09.390
in the title or not, okay?

00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:12.840
And we verified that indeed
this buzzy word variable

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:14.970
is predictive of both the theorem

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:19.110
and it is also predictable
by the paper abstract.

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:22.350
So it's clearly correlated
with both of those things.

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:26.160
And what we do is we simulate
an outcome that depends on

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:28.860
both the theorem and
this buzzy word variable.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:33.270
So in a sense, we create
a confounded outcome,

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:34.740
and then when we go to fit the model,

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:36.813
we don't give it the true confounder Z.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:39.930
We see how well it can recover that

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:42.113
from the text directly, okay?

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:47.160
So let me just show one
of the top line results

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:47.993
from this paper,

00:26:47.993 --> 00:26:49.860
but there's many others in the paper.

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:54.060
So we compare this causal
BERT idea with just an MLP

00:26:54.060 --> 00:26:56.430
that takes the text as input

00:26:56.430 --> 00:26:59.963
and predicts T and also predicts Y, okay?

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:05.220
And we do the causal effect
estimation in the same way

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:07.383
that we do with causal BERT.

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:11.640
And what we do is we vary the dependence

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:12.473
between the confounder

00:27:12.473 --> 00:27:14.310
and outcome with the expectation

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:16.980
as the outcome is super, super confounded,

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:20.430
then it should be harder
to get an unbiased

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:21.693
causal effect estimate.

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:24.750
The ground truth is just the ground truth

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:26.130
causal effect, okay?

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:28.143
So the causal effect is just one.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:32.400
The unadjusted method just
reports the correlation

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:33.900
between treatment and outcome,

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:38.250
and unsurprisingly, it's
very biased as the dependence

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:40.893
between the confounder
and outcome gets high.

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:42.720
The neural network,

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:44.610
which is doing some adjustment

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:46.590
because it's taking the text as input.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:48.690
You can think about it
as like the technique

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.060
that I introduced as the naive technique

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:53.340
that just doesn't do any
representation learning,

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:55.890
that just tries to do
adjustment with a full text.

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:58.260
And indeed, it does better.

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:00.180
But in this particular experiment,

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:02.580
we see that the causal BERT,

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:05.040
these causally sufficient
text representations

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:06.993
led to more accurate effect estimates.

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:11.910
Throughout our experiments,
we saw this theme recurring,

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:15.630
and of course this is
on semisynthetic data,

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:17.040
but this is kind of giving

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:19.293
some initial promising results, right?

00:28:21.810 --> 00:28:25.680
And instead of talking about
more of the results here,

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:27.420
I want to just kind of give the key point,

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:32.420
which is that when we want
to extract confounders

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:35.010
from some high-dimensional
signal like text

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:38.520
or maybe images or medical data,

00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:41.640
then the constraint that we
need for deep learning methods

00:28:41.640 --> 00:28:45.450
is some constraint that prefers
the confounding information

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:49.020
over other generative
factors that are irrelevant

00:28:49.020 --> 00:28:50.370
to the causal problem, okay?

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:52.920
So, so long as we can
separate these two signals,

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:55.260
then deep learning gives us an opportunity

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:57.033
to estimate causal effects.

00:28:58.050 --> 00:29:00.570
Of course, some assumptions
have to be satisfied,

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:04.233
there's a caveat, but
there's an opportunity here.

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:06.870
Now, we should ask then, okay,

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:10.020
well, what about if we
want to use representations

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:14.973
as a way of capturing
the causes of an outcome?

00:29:16.950 --> 00:29:19.650
What do representations
have to satisfy there?

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:21.930
So let me give a motivating
example for this.

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:24.210
Let's say I wanted to
study the causal effect

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:26.010
of some proteins,

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:28.530
some latent macromolecules

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:31.590
on a disease phenotype of interest.

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:33.510
It's pretty hard to measure protein levels

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:36.090
so maybe what we have access to

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:38.190
is gene expression measurements,

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:40.620
which are going to be, in this case,

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:45.600
a parent of the latent
protein levels, okay?

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.730
And we want to ask, can we,

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:51.780
how can we learn a representation
of these latent causes

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:53.970
so that we can then do causal inference

00:29:53.970 --> 00:29:56.870
and understand the effects
that they have on this disease?

00:29:57.960 --> 00:30:00.960
Let's again, for purpose
of building this up,

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:02.670
start with the most naive idea.

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:06.090
So the first thing that
we may think of is, well,

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:09.870
I'll train a feedforward
neural network to predict

00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:13.140
the outcome from some gene
expression measurements.

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:16.230
So here we can think of
the first many layers

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:19.110
of a neural network as
the feature extractor

00:30:19.110 --> 00:30:23.490
that tries to recover the
relevant latent causes,

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:26.433
and then I can maybe train
a linear map to predict why.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:32.400
So here the output of the
feature extractor I will think of

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:34.230
as being my latent, as a proxy,

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:35.940
as a substitute of my latent causes.

00:30:35.940 --> 00:30:36.870
And we should ask,

00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:39.423
does this work for causal inference?

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:44.370
So let's just think what do the solutions

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:47.250
for this representation look like?

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:49.770
Another way of putting this
is what are all the things

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:52.203
that minimizing this
objective would return?

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:55.650
So suppose the true
model really is linear,

00:30:55.650 --> 00:31:00.650
then of course the round
truth representation, right?

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:03.870
The thing that just actually captures Z

00:31:03.870 --> 00:31:07.110
and the true linear map W will
minimize prediction error,

00:31:07.110 --> 00:31:08.610
that's for sure.

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:10.860
But there's actually going to be

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:12.780
infinitely many other solutions, as well.

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:14.340
So, for example,

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:18.390
all these other solutions
that apply some linear

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:22.837
transformation to the features phi and,

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:27.750
or, sorry, that apply linear
transformation to the true Z

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:29.370
and then undo that transformation,

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:32.070
these are all valid
solutions that minimize

00:31:32.070 --> 00:31:33.900
this prediction error.

00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:38.340
And we're very likely to end
up with one of these rotated

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:41.043
representations as opposed
to the ground true sets.

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:43.290
So what does that look like?

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:47.670
So let's just expand out what
this AZ term would look like.

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:51.510
It is something that takes
the true latent causes,

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:53.700
the true protein levels, let's say,

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:55.200
and entangles them.

00:31:55.200 --> 00:31:57.930
It mixes them with each other, right?

00:31:57.930 --> 00:31:59.340
And now let's ask,

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:01.080
can we still do causal inference

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:02.430
with a representation like this?

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:05.700
And it would be fairly clear to see no,

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:07.770
because there's a very big difference

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:10.320
in the causal effect of Z1

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:13.773
and Z1 entangled with all of the other Zs.

00:32:16.140 --> 00:32:20.340
So for causal inference, right?

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:23.040
This naive representation
learning doesn't suffice.

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:26.170
Somehow we need extra
constraints to make sure

00:32:27.165 --> 00:32:30.120
that the semantics of the
causes are maintained,

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:31.920
and we'll see more what
that means formally

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:32.753
in a second here.

00:32:32.753 --> 00:32:35.970
So there's a whole field called

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:38.280
identifiable representation learning

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:43.110
that exactly makes this idea
of preserve semantics precise.

00:32:43.110 --> 00:32:47.460
So what we want is to recover
the true latent causes

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:49.680
up to some transformations
that are very benign

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:51.870
that don't affect our causal conclusions.

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:55.110
In that case it would be permutation.

00:32:55.110 --> 00:32:57.750
So you can change the
order in which you label

00:32:57.750 --> 00:33:02.040
the latent variables or
some rescaling, okay?

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:03.540
So these won't hurt our ability

00:33:03.540 --> 00:33:05.430
to do downstream causal inferences.

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:08.430
And so we would like
to get representations

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:10.950
that actually recover the ground truth

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:12.390
up to these transformations.

00:33:12.390 --> 00:33:14.430
And that's not, as we saw,

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:16.980
possible with these generic

00:33:16.980 --> 00:33:18.960
representation learning objectives.

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:20.310
So what do we need to do?

00:33:20.310 --> 00:33:22.140
We often need other kinds of data

00:33:22.140 --> 00:33:24.900
or constraints that we use to pin down

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:26.250
the correct representation.

00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:30.630
There's a lot of work that has
recently come out around this

00:33:30.630 --> 00:33:32.430
that uses data augmentation,

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:36.570
so paired samples of every single image

00:33:36.570 --> 00:33:38.703
or text that you have in your corpus,

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:41.460
work that uses temporal data

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:43.260
and constraints on the,

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:46.293
like the latent transition
matrix of an HMM, for example.

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:51.630
There's work that uses multiple
domains, AKA environments,

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:53.520
or multiple tasks.

00:33:53.520 --> 00:33:55.950
And there's also work, ours, in fact,

00:33:55.950 --> 00:33:59.070
that is one of these that uses constraints

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:01.920
on the decoding function.

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:03.600
So in this case I just had a linear map,

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:05.883
but there can be other decoding functions.

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:11.430
What I'm going to talk about
briefly is a technique that falls

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:15.210
into this multiple environments
line of work that uses

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:18.900
a very particular
constraint based on sparsity

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:21.930
that will give us
identifiable representations.

00:34:21.930 --> 00:34:24.880
And I'm going to show a little
bit how actually that will

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:28.440
allow us to do valid causal inference.

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:31.530
So we're going to stick with
this linear outcome model,

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:34.590
but we're going to get data

00:34:34.590 --> 00:34:36.480
from many different environments.

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:38.190
So you see I put a plate
around all of this,

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:41.490
which just means that we have
e different environments,

00:34:41.490 --> 00:34:46.260
and in each domain or environment
I will sample X and Y.

00:34:46.260 --> 00:34:47.763
Z is latent.

00:34:49.697 --> 00:34:53.220
So now our model is that
in each environment,

00:34:53.220 --> 00:34:56.220
we're going to have some
environment specific

00:34:56.220 --> 00:34:59.970
linear coefficients that
map the protein levels

00:34:59.970 --> 00:35:02.613
to the disease phenotype Y.

00:35:04.290 --> 00:35:06.060
We're going to make a very
specific choice, though,

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:09.900
on how these per environment Ws,

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:11.640
these linear maps relate to each other.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:14.250
And let me kind of talk you
through that really slowly.

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:17.490
So we're going to assume that
the environments are related

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:19.860
via some known tree.

00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:22.520
So you can think of this
as being, for example,

00:35:22.520 --> 00:35:23.940
in the biological context,

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:27.360
maybe the tree that encodes for speciation

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:29.850
or the phylogenetic tree, AKA,

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:34.850
or a tree that encodes how
cell types differentiated

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:37.770
to be different from each other.

00:35:37.770 --> 00:35:42.770
So what we do is we suppose
that the weights in, let's say,

00:35:42.990 --> 00:35:47.990
environment two are the root node weights

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:52.050
plus some delta offset, okay?

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:55.530
So there's a lot of parameter
sharing happening here.

00:35:55.530 --> 00:35:57.450
As you go further along in the tree,

00:35:57.450 --> 00:35:59.760
so, for example, for W4,

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:03.210
its weights are the root node's weights

00:36:03.210 --> 00:36:07.170
plus delta offsets that
account for all the arcs

00:36:07.170 --> 00:36:09.153
that led to this particular node.

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:17.060
Now, we make a very, very
specific choice on these deltas.

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:18.900
So these deltas are sparse.

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:20.610
They have to be one sparse, in fact,

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:24.420
which means that another way
of putting this is the value of

00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:29.420
a single coefficient can
change and that's it.

00:36:30.390 --> 00:36:33.360
Another way of putting
this is the mechanism

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:35.880
that mediates proteins

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:39.000
and disease progression
varies across environments,

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:44.000
but varies sparsely only
along a single dimension.

00:36:45.660 --> 00:36:50.660
So now the parameters that we
have to learn are going to be

00:36:50.730 --> 00:36:53.910
the root weight, the root node weights,

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:57.030
as well as all of these
offset terms, okay?

00:36:57.030 --> 00:36:59.280
And then we'll collect
all of those offset terms

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:02.190
and put it into a matrix capital delta

00:37:02.190 --> 00:37:05.040
and we'll call the root weights W0, okay?

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:07.200
So those are the parameters
that we have to learn.

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:08.250
And what are we going to do?

00:37:08.250 --> 00:37:12.750
So we call this technique
tree-based regularization

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:14.460
because what it's going to do is,

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:18.600
like our standard
empirical risk objectives,

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:20.040
it is going to minimize

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:22.680
the empirical risk across environments.

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:23.940
So we want to learn weights

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:25.920
that are good at predicting why, right?

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:27.270
That's another way to put it.

00:37:27.270 --> 00:37:32.270
But we penalize these offset
matrix to be sparse, okay?

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:36.180
So we add a penalty that
measures the L0 norm

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:39.870
of this delta matrix so that the offsets

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:41.790
that we're learning
are sparse as possible.

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:43.023
So why does this help?

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:48.630
Let's go back to considering
our entangled solution, right?

00:37:48.630 --> 00:37:53.010
And remember we said to
minimize prediction error,

00:37:53.010 --> 00:37:55.890
what this entangled solution has to do

00:37:55.890 --> 00:37:57.880
is undo the entanglement

00:37:59.250 --> 00:38:01.830
and then it can compose
that with the kind of

00:38:01.830 --> 00:38:04.773
the true weights and offsets.

00:38:05.820 --> 00:38:09.480
But the trick here as to
why this L0 penalty helps us

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:13.590
select a good representation
for our purposes is that

00:38:13.590 --> 00:38:17.040
notice that this matrix multiplication

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:19.293
of delta times A inverse,

00:38:20.130 --> 00:38:22.440
A inverse is not sparse,
it's dense, right?

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:25.200
It's undoing a dense
linear transformation.

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:28.410
Generally this is not
going to achieve a lower L0

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:30.810
than the ground truth sparse solution.

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:32.670
That's the trick here.

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:36.180
So we're adding a constraint
that preferentially

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:38.940
prefers the solutions,

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:41.910
the representations that
we would like to get

00:38:41.910 --> 00:38:46.710
as opposed to the linearly
transformed representations.

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:50.340
And so the theorem in fact is
just stated informally here,

00:38:50.340 --> 00:38:53.400
is that when we have this
tree-based regularization

00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:55.020
with this L0 term,

00:38:55.020 --> 00:38:59.700
the solutions that minimize the L0 norm

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:02.700
are the ones that recover the true latents

00:39:02.700 --> 00:39:05.400
up to permutation and scaling, okay?

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:07.290
So you may shuffle around,

00:39:07.290 --> 00:39:12.090
if originally protein one was protein one

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:13.680
in your learned representation,

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:15.180
you may call it protein three,

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:16.260
but that doesn't really matter.

00:39:16.260 --> 00:39:18.420
That's kind of semantics, right?

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:21.350
And the rescaling, as well,

00:39:21.350 --> 00:39:23.550
is another transformation
that we're kind of,

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:26.010
it's benign for our purposes.

00:39:26.010 --> 00:39:29.970
And now let me kind of
ask this question again.

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:34.970
Does these permutation
invariant representations,

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:39.150
do they really help us
with causal inference?

00:39:39.150 --> 00:39:42.970
And so what we did was we simulated data

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:47.100
and what we did was we simulated data

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:50.640
and we fit this model using two things.

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:53.670
One, just a baseline neural
network like I described

00:39:53.670 --> 00:39:57.960
that simply takes gene
expression pooled across

00:39:57.960 --> 00:39:59.730
all the environments as input,

00:39:59.730 --> 00:40:02.250
predicts, learns a representation phi,

00:40:02.250 --> 00:40:04.113
predicts Y with a linear map.

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:09.900
And we had the representation
learned via our approach,

00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:12.810
and what we do is we use
each of these separately

00:40:12.810 --> 00:40:15.183
to estimate the causal effect on Y,

00:40:16.710 --> 00:40:19.650
and we also do that with the
ground truth representations,

00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:22.470
and we compare the errors,

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:24.540
the differences and
estimates that you would get

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:28.140
via the ground truth
versus our, these three,

00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:30.440
these two different
representation techniques.

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:33.180
The caveats here,

00:40:33.180 --> 00:40:36.990
we don't deal with things
like unobserved confounding.

00:40:36.990 --> 00:40:38.910
We're able to estimate the causal effect

00:40:38.910 --> 00:40:41.250
in a very straightforward
way using causal adjustment

00:40:41.250 --> 00:40:42.900
like I showed earlier.

00:40:42.900 --> 00:40:47.370
And the key takeaway is
that it's kind of very clear

00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:49.620
in this matrix that,

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:51.720
or these results tables,

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:55.630
is that the representation
learned by our approach

00:40:57.180 --> 00:41:00.150
is able to more or less give
you the same causal effect

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:02.730
that you would've gotten if
you'd used the ground truth,

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:04.110
but this entangled,

00:41:04.110 --> 00:41:06.840
this linearly entangled
representation you get when you fit

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:11.280
just a naive neural network
really, really biases

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:13.860
your causal effect estimation, okay?

00:41:13.860 --> 00:41:16.473
So again, the takeaway is just that,

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:21.510
contrast this to the previous
world when we were just trying

00:41:21.510 --> 00:41:24.180
to extract confounding information.

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:26.580
We didn't care about this identifiable

00:41:26.580 --> 00:41:27.750
representation learning, right?

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:30.570
We just cared about
separating the confounders

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:32.190
from everything else.

00:41:32.190 --> 00:41:34.950
But now our desiderata is a bit stronger.

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:36.060
We actually need,

00:41:36.060 --> 00:41:40.620
in order to do causal inference
where we think of the causes

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:44.673
as being learned via
latent representations,

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:47.580
we have a higher standard.

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:51.150
We need to have these
disentangled representations

00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:55.823
that somehow maintains the
semantics of the causes, okay?

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:03.150
Now, I want to kind of
stay within this vein

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:04.320
and ask this question of,

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:08.160
well, how can causal
models help deep learning?

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:10.890
And I alluded to this idea that

00:42:10.890 --> 00:42:13.740
when you combine representation learning,

00:42:13.740 --> 00:42:15.240
deep learning, and causality,

00:42:15.240 --> 00:42:16.950
we may get some nice benefits

00:42:16.950 --> 00:42:18.870
for generalizing to out of distribution.

00:42:18.870 --> 00:42:21.720
And I want to go back to that
point as my concluding thing.

00:42:24.420 --> 00:42:27.870
Let's just remind ourselves the
superpower of causal models,

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:32.430
which is that there's a lot of invariance

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:37.430
that they very nicely encapsulate
where only the variable

00:42:37.980 --> 00:42:39.630
that you're intervening on changes,

00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:41.310
its distribution changes,

00:42:41.310 --> 00:42:43.800
and in this case it changes to a constant,

00:42:43.800 --> 00:42:45.660
but every other parameter,

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:48.310
every other conditional
distribution stays invariant.

00:42:49.472 --> 00:42:50.305
And in some sense,

00:42:50.305 --> 00:42:54.000
this invariance is exactly what
we need when we want to be,

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:56.130
we want to get kind of stable behavior

00:42:56.130 --> 00:42:57.783
across domains, right?

00:42:58.680 --> 00:43:02.670
We want to use causal mechanisms

00:43:02.670 --> 00:43:04.080
to predict as much as possible

00:43:04.080 --> 00:43:06.270
because their behavior remains invariant

00:43:06.270 --> 00:43:11.270
or the risk that they achieve
will remain invariant.

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:14.643
So let me illustrate this
with a more concrete example.

00:43:16.620 --> 00:43:17.820
In this example,

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:21.010
which was taken from a paper
that was using camera traps

00:43:22.020 --> 00:43:24.540
to do object detection,

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:26.940
they had this,

00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:29.250
they noticed this observation that

00:43:29.250 --> 00:43:32.730
when cows are in pasture backgrounds,

00:43:32.730 --> 00:43:35.430
this model clearly recognizes
that there is a cow

00:43:35.430 --> 00:43:36.263
in the image,

00:43:36.263 --> 00:43:40.200
but when there's a cow
frolicking around on a beach,

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:42.690
it notices that there's a
beach and water and seashore,

00:43:42.690 --> 00:43:45.180
but it doesn't recognize
that there's no cow

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:47.160
or that there's a cow, right?

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:49.260
So this was the observation that was made.

00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:52.380
So it seems like there's
some spurious relationship

00:43:52.380 --> 00:43:55.290
between what's predicted
and the background.

00:43:55.290 --> 00:43:59.940
So let me just give a twee model for this.

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:02.730
It may be a little bit
contrived, but that's okay.

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:04.590
It's for example's sake.

00:44:04.590 --> 00:44:07.080
So suppose that this is what's happening,

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:11.100
that there's a human labeler, right?

00:44:11.100 --> 00:44:13.230
How they label is very clearly affected

00:44:13.230 --> 00:44:15.210
by what's in the image,

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:17.190
the animal that's in the image.

00:44:17.190 --> 00:44:19.980
But let's say they're also affected

00:44:19.980 --> 00:44:22.113
by the quality of the image itself.

00:44:23.250 --> 00:44:25.950
So if it's really blurry,
it's hard for them to label.

00:44:25.950 --> 00:44:28.860
And let's say the quality is,

00:44:28.860 --> 00:44:31.380
it confounds, as well, the scene.

00:44:31.380 --> 00:44:34.680
So maybe when you're trying
to photograph animals

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:35.850
on a beach,

00:44:35.850 --> 00:44:39.570
or if you're photographing
them with snow in a blizzard,

00:44:39.570 --> 00:44:42.783
the quality also just
tends to be much worse.

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:46.500
So what we see here,

00:44:46.500 --> 00:44:49.350
and let me just kind
of introduce a very key

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:50.670
other assumption, as well.

00:44:50.670 --> 00:44:53.040
Suppose that between the training domain

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:57.180
and the test domain, the quality,

00:44:57.180 --> 00:44:59.160
the distribution of quality changes.

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:01.500
So in your training data,

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:03.030
let's say you're seeing on average

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:04.830
much more high-quality images.

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:05.760
On your test data,

00:45:05.760 --> 00:45:08.013
you're seeing many more
low-quality images.

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:10.773
In this world,

00:45:12.990 --> 00:45:17.850
what happens is this particular
conditional distribution,

00:45:17.850 --> 00:45:21.243
the probability of predicting
a label given an animal,

00:45:22.260 --> 00:45:24.900
this mechanism will remain the same

00:45:24.900 --> 00:45:29.640
no matter how P quality changes, okay?

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:33.660
Another way to say this
is that it will achieve

00:45:33.660 --> 00:45:34.773
the same risk.

00:45:37.304 --> 00:45:38.760
Or another way to say this actually is

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:39.870
that its risk is bound

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:43.680
and maybe the variance of the
label changes a little bit,

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:47.940
but this particular
mechanisms remains robust.

00:45:47.940 --> 00:45:50.070
But let's consider the
conditional distribution

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:51.810
of label given scene.

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:55.500
This changes as P quality
changes because this very much,

00:45:55.500 --> 00:45:58.350
this association is not causal, right?

00:45:58.350 --> 00:46:01.500
Going back to this idea
that causal conditionals

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:05.130
remain invariant, others do not.

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:06.000
There's no guarantee.

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:07.650
And in this case,

00:46:07.650 --> 00:46:10.230
this conditional can change arbitrarily

00:46:10.230 --> 00:46:11.850
as P quality changes,

00:46:11.850 --> 00:46:14.670
meaning that its out of distribution risk

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:17.760
could be really, really arbitrarily bad.

00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:19.800
So we would have a preference

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:22.260
to learn the causal mechanisms.

00:46:22.260 --> 00:46:23.880
We would have a preference to discover

00:46:23.880 --> 00:46:26.130
the causal parents of a variable

00:46:26.130 --> 00:46:29.250
and use those conditional distributions

00:46:29.250 --> 00:46:30.350
when we do prediction.

00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:35.580
Unfortunately, causal
structure cannot be identified

00:46:35.580 --> 00:46:37.200
from observations alone,

00:46:37.200 --> 00:46:41.400
and the reason for this is
there's nothing separating

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:45.630
just from likelihood alone
this correct causal model

00:46:45.630 --> 00:46:49.080
versus this alternate model in which scene

00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:52.980
could be thought of as a parent
of label, as well, right?

00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:56.430
So this other model in
red is clearly incorrect,

00:46:56.430 --> 00:46:58.020
but it has the same exact,

00:46:58.020 --> 00:47:00.670
it produces the same likelihood
as the correct model.

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:07.760
And so there's been a lot
of work that talks about

00:47:08.280 --> 00:47:10.680
the use of auxiliary data,

00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:13.890
in particular some
environment labels once again.

00:47:13.890 --> 00:47:17.280
So let's say now we also
label our data from the region

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:18.840
that we get the data from

00:47:18.840 --> 00:47:20.970
and let's say region affects the quality

00:47:20.970 --> 00:47:22.440
of the images taken.

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:24.120
And we also have this,

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:26.790
so we have this auxiliary region variable,

00:47:26.790 --> 00:47:30.270
and now our causal model may be identified

00:47:30.270 --> 00:47:34.200
because we notice here that the scene

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:36.750
and region are associated,

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:39.843
but animal and region
marginally are not associated.

00:47:41.280 --> 00:47:44.580
There's no pathway
marginally that takes you

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:45.990
from animal to region.

00:47:45.990 --> 00:47:47.910
So now there's a signal,

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:51.690
there's an asymmetry that we
can use to constrain learning

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:54.270
so that we learn the
causal parents of label

00:47:54.270 --> 00:47:57.930
as opposed to these
spuriously related variables.

00:47:57.930 --> 00:48:01.770
And so there's a lot of
work on this subtopic,

00:48:01.770 --> 00:48:04.590
and I unfortunately, you know,

00:48:04.590 --> 00:48:08.220
would have to spend another
hour to dive into it.

00:48:08.220 --> 00:48:11.350
But the key takeaway here is that

00:48:13.260 --> 00:48:18.150
there's a nice symmetry
between the invariances

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:20.010
that causal models give us

00:48:20.010 --> 00:48:23.640
and how they can be really
useful for predicting

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:27.213
when we use deep learning
methods or any other method.

00:48:28.590 --> 00:48:30.840
Unfortunately, there's
a technical challenge,

00:48:30.840 --> 00:48:34.860
which is that learning causal
models is not identified,

00:48:34.860 --> 00:48:36.510
but with some auxiliary data

00:48:36.510 --> 00:48:39.360
and the causal model
can give us constraints

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:44.360
that may help us learn more
robust predictors, okay?

00:48:46.710 --> 00:48:50.010
So what I'm going to conclude
with is just a lot of

00:48:50.010 --> 00:48:53.640
open problems that I
didn't touch upon today,

00:48:53.640 --> 00:48:57.633
but are also just sort of
good areas of future research.

00:49:00.540 --> 00:49:04.233
I think when we talk about
reinforcement learning,

00:49:05.370 --> 00:49:09.630
there's a lot of work to
do on the perspective of

00:49:09.630 --> 00:49:12.270
embodied agents that
interact with the world

00:49:12.270 --> 00:49:16.500
and have to do interventions
in order to learn

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:17.940
presumably causal variables

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:19.560
and causal models of the world.

00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:21.090
So there's a lot of rich work to do

00:49:21.090 --> 00:49:22.740
in combining reinforcement learning

00:49:22.740 --> 00:49:24.490
and causal representation learning.

00:49:26.520 --> 00:49:28.140
In a similar way,

00:49:28.140 --> 00:49:32.280
we want to understand how
causality could actually improve

00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:36.003
some of the guarantees of
RL, the robustness of RL.

00:49:39.030 --> 00:49:42.150
Another kind of useful thing
about causality is we said

00:49:42.150 --> 00:49:44.760
that it helps us predict the
effects of interventions.

00:49:44.760 --> 00:49:47.850
And it would be very
interesting to have models

00:49:47.850 --> 00:49:49.470
that could predict the effects of

00:49:49.470 --> 00:49:52.470
completely unseen interventions, right?

00:49:52.470 --> 00:49:54.993
And this is kind of very, very open work.

00:49:56.040 --> 00:49:58.530
And then finally, just in terms
of representation learning

00:49:58.530 --> 00:50:01.500
and thinking about really
rich models of science

00:50:01.500 --> 00:50:02.940
and the world,

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:06.150
there's a lot of latent
variables that are really, really

00:50:06.150 --> 00:50:09.480
hard to recover using
representation learning.

00:50:09.480 --> 00:50:12.480
So what I call second order variables,

00:50:12.480 --> 00:50:15.240
like if you have, for example,

00:50:15.240 --> 00:50:17.880
a video or a series of images,

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:20.400
it's not obvious that
we can recover variables

00:50:20.400 --> 00:50:22.620
like the acceleration of the objects

00:50:22.620 --> 00:50:24.330
or the mass of the objects.

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:26.280
We would need some extra assumptions,

00:50:26.280 --> 00:50:28.950
but these kinds of variables
would make our understanding

00:50:28.950 --> 00:50:30.900
of the world much richer.

00:50:30.900 --> 00:50:33.540
So there's a lot of kind of
really interesting work to do

00:50:33.540 --> 00:50:36.330
at the intersection of deep learning RL

00:50:36.330 --> 00:50:37.593
as well as causality.

00:50:38.790 --> 00:50:41.400
And so that's my talk.

00:50:41.400 --> 00:50:42.233
I'm going to conclude,

00:50:42.233 --> 00:50:46.110
and I'll just say that
there's a lot of resources

00:50:46.110 --> 00:50:48.480
that I flashed by in this talk.

00:50:48.480 --> 00:50:53.190
And what I'm going to do to
aid lookup later on is post

00:50:53.190 --> 00:50:55.380
all of these to a resource
section on my website

00:50:55.380 --> 00:50:57.570
later today, I promise.

00:50:57.570 --> 00:51:00.330
So please go have a look
if you want to read about

00:51:00.330 --> 00:51:02.820
outer distribution generalization,

00:51:02.820 --> 00:51:05.760
identifiable representation learning,

00:51:05.760 --> 00:51:07.110
or any of these other topics.

00:51:07.110 --> 00:51:10.623
So thank you so much, and
yeah, I'll take questions.

